WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.462 --> 00:00:04.142
It is a privilege to be a detective.

00:00:05.585 --> 00:00:08.907
You deal with lots and lots of horrible things that comes with the territory.

00:00:10.708 --> 00:00:12.570
The majority of us don't have to see these things,

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:13.570
don't have to deal with these things.

00:00:15.852 --> 00:00:19.815
The launch of a fifth murder inquiry in such a short space of time.

00:00:19.896 --> 00:00:23.158
Justice for the victims is absolutely the most important thing.

00:00:23.719 --> 00:00:27.181
You've been arrested on suspicion of attempted murder and murder.

00:00:27.182 --> 00:00:28.544
It could be worse.

00:00:31.076 --> 00:00:36.320
We can only imagine the grief of the families who lost their loved ones in such brutal circumstances.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:37.641
With your DNA,

00:00:38.262 --> 00:00:40.023
how can that be?

00:00:40.024 --> 00:00:40.463
No comment.

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:46.908
Judge said the murder showed a degree of premeditation and planning.

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:48.250
He'd been stabbed and beaten.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.472
He was very much a madhunt at that stage of the investigation.

00:00:51.813 --> 00:00:53.755
He knew that the wars were closing in on him.

00:00:54.676 --> 00:00:57.338
I always remember a more experienced officer telling me,

00:00:57.838 --> 00:00:58.078
you know,

00:00:58.319 --> 00:00:59.900
you never forget these jobs and...

00:01:00.344 --> 00:01:00.943
He was right.

00:01:02.424 --> 00:01:07.388
These are the detectives who solved some of Britain's most notorious homicides.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.492
Now they relive those cases.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:16.034
Every fear that I had was being realised in front of my eyes.

00:01:16.594 --> 00:01:19.777
Through every twist and turn of the investigation...

00:01:20.138 --> 00:01:23.159
The only way you forget about it is when you're asleep.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:29.584
...their personal accounts reveal what it really takes to catch a killer and bring them...

00:01:29.664 --> 00:01:30.445
to justice.

00:01:30.766 --> 00:01:31.647
As a detective,

00:01:31.927 --> 00:01:32.188
you know,

00:01:32.608 --> 00:01:39.918
there can't be any greater honour than for you to investigate the death or murder of another human being.

00:01:59.628 --> 00:02:04.272
I got to a stage in my career where I knew I had about seven years before I was going to retire.

00:02:04.873 --> 00:02:08.216
And I always wanted to be on what was called the serious crime command.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.683
The opportunity came for me to go onto a murder team and I took it with both hands.

00:02:21.176 --> 00:02:28.740
But it's not every day that as a murder detective that you have the opportunity to investigate a case that's got this much to deal with.

00:02:32.482 --> 00:02:35.563
The brutality of the murders is not something you come across every day.

00:02:37.844 --> 00:02:41.426
Not only were they killed but they were then dismembered and some of those remains were never found.

00:02:44.167 --> 00:02:45.568
It had an international aspect.

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:52.501
This was one that we were never going to see again in our lifetime as murder detectives.

00:03:16.707 --> 00:03:16.828
The

00:03:16.847 --> 00:03:17.828
case began for me.

00:03:18.307 --> 00:03:21.431
In the winter of 2007,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.753
when we received a phone call from the cold case team in Rotterdam,

00:03:27.074 --> 00:03:35.622
they alerted us to the fact that they were going to be reinvestigating the murder of an unidentified female that was recovered from the canal.

00:03:36.764 --> 00:03:43.028
That phone call from Holland was to kick-start one of the most complex inquiries Detective Groves had ever led,

00:03:43.688 --> 00:03:46.312
involving two murders in two countries.

00:03:46.771 --> 00:03:51.134
11 years apart.

00:03:51.135 --> 00:03:52.415
It was going all the way back to

00:03:52.776 --> 00:04:01.981
1990. It was moving all the way forward to 2001 with another person killed and then it lands on our lap in 2007.

00:04:01.983 --> 00:04:05.784
In 1990,

00:04:06.305 --> 00:04:10.807
Dutch police couldn't identify a dismembered body found in a Rotterdam canal.

00:04:11.588 --> 00:04:13.129
The head and hands were missing,

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:16.031
making identification impossible at the time.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:21.720
Seventeen years on,

00:04:22.181 --> 00:04:26.802
updates to the DNA database mean the Dutch hope to be able to name the victim.

00:04:27.603 --> 00:04:28.463
And in doing so,

00:04:28.903 --> 00:04:32.345
offer the Met Police the key to solving their own cold case.

00:04:33.125 --> 00:04:40.348
That of another young woman found dumped in a London canal in 2001.

00:04:40.349 --> 00:04:43.770
My team had originally dealt with the 2001 murder investigation,

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:49.352
when the dismembered and mutilated remains of a female were recovered in London.

00:04:49.728 --> 00:04:55.555
Regions Canal India 98,

00:04:55.574 --> 00:04:57.096
India 98,

00:04:57.098 --> 00:04:57.736
receiving UK Yes sir,

00:04:57.737 --> 00:05:00.000
I've got a...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.504
Two witnesses who say they've seen the packages in the water about half an hour ago.

00:05:06.505 --> 00:05:09.687
So they were fishing with the lads who picked their fish out.

00:05:09.689 --> 00:05:09.848
Proceed,

00:05:09.848 --> 00:05:10.288
I'll pass the answer,

00:05:10.288 --> 00:05:12.850
thank you.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:21.757
There were at least two boys who were fishing on the canal and they noticed that there was something floating within it,

00:05:22.457 --> 00:05:24.660
like a hold-all bag.

00:05:27.704 --> 00:05:35.269
They managed to pull it from the water and straight away they noticed that there was blood seeping from within the bag.

00:05:38.131 --> 00:05:38.792
Once opened,

00:05:38.951 --> 00:05:43.435
there were obviously the mutilated remains of a female.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:55.864
They recovered at least five or six holdalls which contained different body parts.

00:05:58.668 --> 00:06:03.550
They were able to very quickly identify her through DNA.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.071
They would have taken blood samples.

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:07.572
So within a matter of days,

00:06:08.372 --> 00:06:10.733
they had identified that she was Paula Field.

00:06:13.254 --> 00:06:14.415
Paula Fields was a

00:06:14.955 --> 00:06:18.296
31-year-old Liverpudlian who'd been in London for two years.

00:06:21.658 --> 00:06:25.039
Police divers are still searching the Regent's Canal near Camden,

00:06:25.399 --> 00:06:27.200
although six bags have been found.

00:06:27.379 --> 00:06:29.100
not all of her body has been recovered.

00:06:29.521 --> 00:06:35.362
This has raised speculation that the killer kept them as a trophy or an attempt to stop her being identified.

00:06:36.004 --> 00:06:38.145
Some of the bags used were identical to these.

00:06:38.465 --> 00:06:42.547
They were weighed down with bricks and the dismembered body had been wrapped in bin liners.

00:06:52.191 --> 00:06:54.612
Detectives had quickly identified Paula.

00:06:55.307 --> 00:06:57.710
And they strongly believed they knew her killer too.

00:06:58.451 --> 00:06:59.771
But in 2001,

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:02.434
there was not enough evidence to bring him to trial.

00:07:03.555 --> 00:07:05.456
Now all that could be about to change,

00:07:05.877 --> 00:07:12.223
with the reinvestigation of the murder committed 11 years earlier and 200 miles away in Rotterdam,

00:07:12.543 --> 00:07:13.004
Holland.

00:07:23.259 --> 00:07:25.341
A few weeks after the initial phone call,

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:32.983
Detective Groves is notified that Dutch police have formally identified their body as that of Melissa Halstead,

00:07:33.483 --> 00:07:37.725
an American photographer and model last known to be living in Amsterdam.

00:07:40.786 --> 00:07:43.588
If someone is found and not identified,

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:45.129
their remains cannot be cremated.

00:07:46.569 --> 00:07:51.072
The Forensic Institute in Amsterdam had to exhume the remains.

00:07:52.987 --> 00:07:54.628
Once further tests were done,

00:07:55.489 --> 00:08:00.052
they were able to prove without any doubt that that in fact was Melissa Halstead.

00:08:00.612 --> 00:08:04.096
So that opened up a completely different area for the investigation team.

00:08:05.296 --> 00:08:08.338
Dutch police had made extensive efforts to find Melissa,

00:08:08.899 --> 00:08:10.859
but they'd been looking in the wrong place.

00:08:15.082 --> 00:08:17.484
She was reported missing by her family,

00:08:18.465 --> 00:08:20.966
under the assumption that she was living in Amsterdam.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:25.662
So the police couldn't make a link with the remains that were found in Rotterdam.

00:08:28.324 --> 00:08:29.204
Those remains,

00:08:29.305 --> 00:08:30.045
like Paula's,

00:08:30.425 --> 00:08:31.867
were deeply disturbing.

00:08:33.868 --> 00:08:37.090
The police found a grey duffel bag,

00:08:37.831 --> 00:08:39.231
sort of like an army bag,

00:08:39.491 --> 00:08:41.133
floating in the West Asinco Canal.

00:08:44.495 --> 00:08:49.960
They then realised that it actually contained the dismembered and mutilated remains of a female.

00:08:54.423 --> 00:08:57.407
Her head and her hands to this date have never been found.

00:09:03.673 --> 00:09:06.175
A murder investigation was launched at that time,

00:09:06.596 --> 00:09:09.177
but that case remained unsolved.

00:09:09.478 --> 00:09:11.120
There was nothing else to distinguish her.

00:09:12.862 --> 00:09:19.447
The manner and method of Melissa's disposal in Rotterdam almost exactly replicate Paula's in London.

00:09:27.967 --> 00:09:28.887
In both cases,

00:09:29.388 --> 00:09:31.128
the head and the hands had been removed,

00:09:31.488 --> 00:09:33.249
and the case of Paula also her feet.

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:37.791
Once they were dismembered,

00:09:37.831 --> 00:09:42.813
they had been placed in either hole dolls or a duffel bag and deposited in a canal.

00:09:45.654 --> 00:09:48.115
As well as the gruesome manner of their disposal,

00:09:48.615 --> 00:09:55.018
there is another critical factor linking the two cold cases of Paula Fields and Melissa Halstead.

00:09:56.799 --> 00:09:57.159
Melissa...

00:09:57.379 --> 00:09:59.691
had a relationship prior to her death.

00:10:00.844 --> 00:10:04.086
And that relationship was with an individual by the name of John Patrick Sweeney.

00:10:08.989 --> 00:10:10.428
We knew for a fact that

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:16.812
Paula Fields was also in a relationship with John Sweeney when she was killed.

00:10:22.895 --> 00:10:29.680
So then the next thing we asked ourselves was what are the chances of an individual having two girlfriends and they're both...

00:10:30.252 --> 00:10:32.913
Found under the circumstances that those two women were found.

00:10:38.358 --> 00:10:40.158
In London and in Rotterdam,

00:10:40.239 --> 00:10:40.658
Holland,

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.961
detectives have reopened the investigation of two cold case murders.

00:10:45.942 --> 00:10:49.644
Paula Fields and Melissa Halstead were killed in different countries,

00:10:49.805 --> 00:10:50.906
11 years apart.

00:10:54.389 --> 00:10:54.729
Both,

00:10:54.928 --> 00:10:55.668
when they died,

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:59.072
were in a relationship with a man called John Patrick Sweeney.

00:10:59.511 --> 00:11:01.192
a carpenter from Merseyside.

00:11:02.153 --> 00:11:05.076
With the same brutal suspect in both cold cases,

00:11:05.535 --> 00:11:09.479
Detective Howard Groves moves to bring the inquiries under one roof.

00:11:12.461 --> 00:11:17.124
We had to make some decisions at that point as to who would take jurisdiction of those cases.

00:11:20.086 --> 00:11:20.986
My view was,

00:11:21.067 --> 00:11:23.048
and with the consideration of the Dutch,

00:11:23.489 --> 00:11:27.831
is that we were going to try and investigate both matters as one investigation.

00:11:30.371 --> 00:11:41.654
The reason was there's an act of Parliament which basically says that if a UK citizen is suspected of murder or manslaughter committed anywhere in the world,

00:11:42.415 --> 00:11:46.456
that they can be dealt with for that offence in the United Kingdom.

00:11:48.417 --> 00:11:53.438
So that was a selling point to the Dutch that we could deal with the matter,

00:11:53.658 --> 00:11:57.379
whereas they couldn't deal with our matter in the Netherlands.

00:11:58.839 --> 00:12:00.740
The arrangement is groundbreaking.

00:12:02.401 --> 00:12:11.507
It was the first of its kind where a UK police service and a foreign police service would be investigating a case of murder.

00:12:13.968 --> 00:12:15.549
With the support of Dutch police,

00:12:15.951 --> 00:12:20.472
Detective Howard Groves is now leading not one cold case murder investigation,

00:12:20.973 --> 00:12:21.493
but two.

00:12:27.008 --> 00:12:27.728
In a cold case,

00:12:27.788 --> 00:12:29.249
you're obviously going back historically.

00:12:29.269 --> 00:12:34.490
You're having to go back in time and find out an awful lot about witnesses,

00:12:34.909 --> 00:12:36.531
what evidence had been gathered,

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:41.632
and then try and bring that evidence now to bear in a subsequent investigation.

00:12:42.432 --> 00:12:46.153
Pathological reports from both cold cases are compared.

00:12:49.774 --> 00:12:55.056
Hundreds of witnesses in Holland and London who gave evidence when Melissa and Paula disappeared

00:12:55.488 --> 00:12:58.149
are interviewed again to confirm their evidence.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:04.012
You are having to identify witnesses who would have been spoken to in 1990,

00:13:04.992 --> 00:13:08.093
witnesses that would have been spoken to in 2001.

00:13:08.094 --> 00:13:13.576
You would find that some people would not be able to recollect some of the information that they provided you at that time.

00:13:15.196 --> 00:13:16.317
Just on the Dutch case alone,

00:13:16.477 --> 00:13:21.179
there were thousands and thousands of items that we had to reintroduce into the investigation.

00:13:22.639 --> 00:13:24.040
All of the items that were...

00:13:24.503 --> 00:13:25.384
Completed in

00:13:25.724 --> 00:13:27.385
Holland, had to be translated,

00:13:28.386 --> 00:13:33.951
and we had to make it so that it was going to be evidentially sound within the UK court of law.

00:13:36.913 --> 00:13:38.334
In order to build his case,

00:13:38.755 --> 00:13:44.961
Howard and his team revisit all that is known about John Sweeney's relationships with Paula and Melissa.

00:13:47.141 --> 00:13:53.086
We had evidence of assaults that he committed against Melissa and was convicted on at least three occasions for...

00:13:53.968 --> 00:14:11.456
common assaults actual bodily harm on her so all that evidence was useful for us to have to build up a picture about the nature of the individual that he was melissa met sweeney in london in 1986 he

00:14:11.497 --> 00:14:23.384
was violent from the start and twice found guilty of assaulting her each time he was fined five pounds and bound over to keep the peace later in austria

00:14:23.664 --> 00:14:25.145
where she'd fled to escape him,

00:14:25.545 --> 00:14:27.147
he brutally attacked her again,

00:14:27.347 --> 00:14:27.847
twice.

00:14:29.448 --> 00:14:32.070
This time he spent four months in police custody,

00:14:32.471 --> 00:14:33.471
awaiting trial.

00:14:35.053 --> 00:14:36.614
When she arrived in Vienna,

00:14:37.274 --> 00:14:44.720
he had obviously tracked her down and it would appear that she was telling him she didn't want a relationship with him.

00:14:46.841 --> 00:14:50.184
So he forces her into the flat and that's when she's attacked with a hammer.

00:14:53.495 --> 00:14:54.376
Fortunately for her,

00:14:54.836 --> 00:14:56.177
a friend comes to her aid,

00:14:56.637 --> 00:14:58.339
and Sweeney's actually arrested,

00:14:58.679 --> 00:14:59.680
hiding in the property,

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:04.263
and he's subsequently charged and convicted for an assault on her.

00:15:06.264 --> 00:15:08.066
But despite Sweeney's violence,

00:15:08.406 --> 00:15:11.328
Melissa made an astonishing plea in his defence.

00:15:12.669 --> 00:15:17.553
We were quite surprised that Melissa decided to approach the court and ask for lenience,

00:15:19.274 --> 00:15:20.695
and he is obviously then deported.

00:15:23.724 --> 00:15:24.664
But unfortunately,

00:15:24.804 --> 00:15:27.008
they find themselves both in Amsterdam.

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:31.774
Melissa was trying to get away from him,

00:15:32.674 --> 00:15:33.677
but at the same time,

00:15:33.996 --> 00:15:36.639
she was sucked back into a relationship with him,

00:15:37.782 --> 00:15:39.443
and that was our ultimate downfall.

00:15:50.627 --> 00:15:53.468
To further understand Sweeney's history of violence,

00:15:53.928 --> 00:15:56.529
Melissa's family in America is re-interviewed.

00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:10.216
Her sister was contacted once the reinvestigation started because we were aware that she may be able to provide some corroborative evidence in relation to assaults that she would have witnessed when she visited her sister in London.

00:16:12.356 --> 00:16:15.538
Her sister basically described Sweeney as controlling.

00:16:17.399 --> 00:16:20.059
She actually saw some bruises on Melissa.

00:16:20.503 --> 00:16:22.429
And I'd ask her where she'd received those from,

00:16:22.469 --> 00:16:25.539
and she indicated that they were from an attack from John Sweeney.

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:29.731
To her sister,

00:16:30.191 --> 00:16:33.134
Melissa's appeals for clemency remain a mystery.

00:16:35.756 --> 00:16:47.746
She expressed her concern as to why Melissa would think it smart to actually approach the courts and try and get some leniency in respect to John Sweeney.

00:16:49.107 --> 00:16:52.810
Soon after Sweeney's release in March 1989,

00:16:52.811 --> 00:16:55.172
the sisters shared an important phone call.

00:16:56.996 --> 00:16:57.356
She said,

00:16:57.436 --> 00:16:57.616
look,

00:16:57.656 --> 00:16:58.837
if I ever go missing,

00:16:59.397 --> 00:17:00.397
you'll know who's done it,

00:17:00.798 --> 00:17:02.698
which was damning evidence in many regards.

00:17:03.138 --> 00:17:03.919
Melissa is saying,

00:17:03.939 --> 00:17:04.139
look,

00:17:04.659 --> 00:17:06.500
if I go missing and you can't find me,

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:08.661
you know who's done it.

00:17:09.121 --> 00:17:09.821
It's going to be him.

00:17:11.822 --> 00:17:12.702
Eight months later,

00:17:13.203 --> 00:17:15.944
Melissa made what would be her last call home.

00:17:17.044 --> 00:17:18.705
In November 1989,

00:17:18.706 --> 00:17:19.825
she would have been ringing up her mother,

00:17:20.065 --> 00:17:21.246
but her mother wasn't at home,

00:17:21.786 --> 00:17:23.127
so she obviously left a message.

00:17:23.128 --> 00:17:23.187
Hi,

00:17:23.188 --> 00:17:23.247
Mom,

00:17:23.248 --> 00:17:23.487
it's...

00:17:27.248 --> 00:17:28.709
It's Melissa,

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:30.070
I just called to wish you happy birthday.

00:17:30.071 --> 00:17:31.271
I'm going to be getting a fax machine sometime soon,

00:17:31.272 --> 00:17:32.272
so I'll call and that number with you anyway.

00:17:32.273 --> 00:17:34.374
Have a good time and take care of yourself.

00:17:34.375 --> 00:17:34.734
Goodbye,

00:17:34.735 --> 00:17:36.855
I love you.

00:17:41.139 --> 00:17:45.702
That was her last contact as far as her family were concerned,

00:17:45.822 --> 00:17:48.104
wishing her mother a happy birthday.

00:17:55.116 --> 00:17:58.919
Alongside Sweeney's turbulent romance with Melissa in Europe,

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:04.183
Detective Groves is investigating his relationship with Paula Fields in London.

00:18:05.816 --> 00:18:07.436
Paula Field was from Liverpool.

00:18:07.917 --> 00:18:08.657
She came to

00:18:09.177 --> 00:18:10.717
London, I think,

00:18:10.797 --> 00:18:13.298
initially with one of her children,

00:18:14.318 --> 00:18:18.019
and I believe that later on another child came to live with her.

00:18:18.159 --> 00:18:19.840
But because of her chaotic lifestyle,

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:22.180
she couldn't look after the children and they were taken into care.

00:18:23.021 --> 00:18:26.422
So she was therefore then involved in prostitution,

00:18:26.702 --> 00:18:27.462
taking drugs,

00:18:28.062 --> 00:18:34.584
and she would have at some point in sort of mid-2000 formed a relationship with...

00:18:35.444 --> 00:18:36.044
John Sweeney.

00:18:40.507 --> 00:18:41.207
At the time,

00:18:41.567 --> 00:18:43.889
Sweeney was living in a flat in North London.

00:18:45.509 --> 00:18:46.730
His brother was a neighbour.

00:18:49.111 --> 00:18:52.393
Sweeney certainly alleged that she had stolen money from him on occasion.

00:18:53.414 --> 00:18:54.734
She had stolen his cigarettes,

00:18:55.315 --> 00:18:56.535
stolen his mobile phone,

00:18:56.536 --> 00:18:57.076
so he was...

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:00.641
A lot of times quite annoyed with her.

00:19:00.941 --> 00:19:04.062
And when she would go out and get drugs from her drug dealers,

00:19:04.322 --> 00:19:05.143
she would sometimes say,

00:19:05.203 --> 00:19:05.883
put it on the tab,

00:19:06.163 --> 00:19:07.423
and he would pay for it.

00:19:09.264 --> 00:19:13.966
Some tenants said they actually remember seeing her actually playing her trade close to where

00:19:14.306 --> 00:19:14.906
Sweeney lived.

00:19:16.486 --> 00:19:17.547
And obviously that annoyed him,

00:19:17.567 --> 00:19:20.048
so I think he eventually saw her as a bit of an irritant.

00:19:21.428 --> 00:19:23.330
And then all of a sudden in December of

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:27.713
